            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-15588",
              "name": "OsPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "GDBusServer: glib2: GDBusServer pre-authentication DoS via unbounded SASL line buffering"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A denial-of-service and resource exhaustion vulnerability exists within the `GDBus` component of GLib. The `gdbusauth` authentication mechanism fails to enforce proper length limitations on data lines read from a client. An unauthenticated local or remote attacker can exploit this lack of input validation by sending excessively long streams of data, causing the application to consume massive amounts of system memory and CPU, potentially leading to a crash or system hang."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-15588",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-15588\nSeverity: MEDIUM\nPackage: libglib2.0-data\nFixed Version: \nLink: [CVE-2026-15588](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-15588)\nA denial-of-service and resource exhaustion vulnerability exists within the `GDBus` component of GLib. The `gdbusauth` authentication mechanism fails to enforce proper length limitations on data lines read from a client. An unauthenticated local or remote attacker can exploit this lack of input validation by sending excessively long streams of data, causing the application to consume massive amounts of system memory and CPU, potentially leading to a crash or system hang.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-15588**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|MEDIUM|libglib2.0-data||[CVE-2026-15588](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-15588)|\n\nA denial-of-service and resource exhaustion vulnerability exists within the `GDBus` component of GLib. The `gdbusauth` authentication mechanism fails to enforce proper length limitations on data lines read from a client. An unauthenticated local or remote attacker can exploit this lack of input validation by sending excessively long streams of data, causing the application to consume massive amounts of system memory and CPU, potentially leading to a crash or system hang."
              },
              "properties": {
                "precision": "very-high",
                "security-severity": "5.5",
                "tags": [
